DATA={1: ('Whole class: statistic or parameter', '23/35=.6571=65.71%. PARAMETER IF THIS WHOLE CLASS IS THE TARGET POPULATION. STATISTIC IF THE CLASS IS A SAMPLE USED TO DESCRIBE A LARGER POPULATION. DEFINE THE TARGET FIRST.', 'basic,parameter'), 2: ('Building stories: discrete', '22 STORIES IS A COUNT: DISCRETE, NOT CONTINUOUS.', 'measurement'), 3: ('Sample 100 of 800 teachers', 'RANDOM: NUMBER ALL800; RANDOMLY PICK100 DISTINCT NUMBERS. SYSTEMATIC: RANDOM START1..8 THEN EVERY8TH TEACHER. CONVENIENCE:100 EASIEST TO REACH. STRATIFIED: DIVIDE INTO RELEVANT GROUPS; RANDOMLY SAMPLE FROM EVERY GROUP. PROPORTIONAL ALLOCATION=100*GROUP SIZE/800, ADJUST TO TOTAL100. CLUSTER: ONE VALID DESIGN PARTITIONS800 INTO8 GROUPS OF100, RANDOMLY CHOOSES ONE GROUP, AND SURVEYS ALL100 IN IT. NATURAL SCHOOL CLUSTERS MAY HAVE UNEQUAL SIZES AND WILL NOT AUTOMATICALLY YIELD100.', 'sampling'), 4: ('Compare test scores / z axes', 'A:x44,MEAN40,SD4 -> z1. B:x283.4,MEAN260,SD26 -> z.9. A HAS HIGHER RELATIVE POSITION. z=-3..3 MATCHES A:28,32,36,40,44,48,52; B:182,208,234,260,286,312,338. DRAW BOTH CURVES AND MARK THE SCORES.', 'relative'), 5: ('Gym age: usual range', 'MEAN40,SD14. USUAL12..68. 72 IS UNUSUALLY HIGH; z=2.2857. USE STRICT OUTSIDE +/-2SD.', 'normal'), 6: ('Men / women pulse lists', 'MEN120,77,89,97,124,68,72,96. WOMEN115,86,49,56,78,76,78,95. USE SAMPLE VARIANCE. MEN:MEAN92.875,SD20.8836,VAR436.125,MIN68,Q1=74.5,MEDIAN92.5,Q3=108.5,MAX124,MODE DNE,RANGE56,MIDRANGE96,IQR34. WOMEN:MEAN79.125,SD20.8425,VAR434.4107,MIN49,Q1=66,MEDIAN78,Q3=90.5,MAX115,MODE78,RANGE66,MIDRANGE82,IQR24.5. NO IQR OUTLIERS. DRAW BOXES ON SAME AXIS. MEN HAVE HIGHER MEAN/MEDIAN; SDs ARE NEARLY EQUAL. MEN HAVE LARGER IQR BUT WOMEN HAVE LARGER RANGE. DO NOT CLAIM EVERY SPREAD MEASURE IS LARGER FOR ONE GROUP.', 'compare'), 7: ('Same mean and mode, different spread', 'LIST A:8,10,10,10,12. LIST B:0,10,10,10,20. BOTH MEAN10 AND MODE10. RANGES4 AND20. CENTER ALONE DOES NOT DESCRIBE SPREAD OR SHAPE. COMPARE BOTH LISTS TO VERIFY.', 'compare'), 8: ('Unusual z and empirical rule', 'FOR ROUGHLY NORMAL DATA, ABOUT95% IS WITHIN2SD, SO ABOUT5% IS OUTSIDE:2.5% EACH TAIL. UNUSUAL z<-2 OR z>2. THIS PERCENT IS NOT GUARANTEED FOR ARBITRARY SHAPES.', 'empirical'), 9: ('Relative frequency / large numbers', 'AS INDEPENDENT TRIALS WITH THE SAME PROBABILITY INCREASE, OBSERVED RELATIVE FREQUENCY TENDS TOWARD THE THEORETICAL PROBABILITY. FINITE SAMPLES CAN DIFFER. NO GUARANTEE OF EXACT EQUALITY; MORE DATA DOES NOT FIX BIASED SAMPLING.', 'lln'), 10: ('Select a woman', '69 WOMEN AND68 MEN: TOTAL137. P(WOMAN)=69/137=.5036.', 'basic')}

